insert into questions (id, unit, topic, concept, stem, choices, answer, why) values
  ('FI01', 'Fixed Income', 'Bond Features', 'Zero-coupon structure', 'A zero-coupon bond differs from a coupon-paying bond in that its return to an investor who holds it to maturity comes entirely from:', '["Periodic coupon payments reinvested at the original yield to maturity","A floating coupon rate that resets with a reference rate","The difference between the purchase price and the face value paid at maturity"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Zero-coupon bonds pay no periodic interest; they are issued at a discount to face value, and the investor''s entire return is the gradual accretion from the purchase price up to face value at maturity. The reinvested-coupons choice describes a coupon bond''s yield-to-maturity assumption, which cannot apply when there are no coupons to reinvest, and the floating-rate choice describes a different instrument (a floating-rate note) entirely.'),
  ('FI02', 'Fixed Income', 'Bond Features', 'Call provision benefit', 'A callable bond gives the issuer the right, but not the obligation, to redeem the bond before maturity. This feature is most valuable to the issuer when:', '["Interest rates have fallen since issuance, allowing the issuer to refinance at a lower cost","Interest rates have risen since issuance, allowing the issuer to lock in the original coupon","The issuer''s credit rating has been downgraded, increasing its borrowing costs"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A call option lets the issuer retire high-coupon debt and reissue at the new, lower prevailing rate once rates fall, just like a homeowner refinancing a mortgage. If rates have instead risen, the issuer is already paying a below-market coupon and has no incentive to call and refinance at a higher rate, and a downgrade raises the cost of any new debt the issuer might issue, making a call less attractive, not more.'),
  ('FI03', 'Fixed Income', 'Bond Features', 'Put provision benefit', 'A putable bond gives the bondholder the right to sell the bond back to the issuer at a predetermined price before maturity. This feature is most valuable to the investor when:', '["Interest rates have fallen, letting the investor redeem early and reinvest at the new, lower rates","The issuer''s credit rating has improved, raising the bond''s market price above the put price","Interest rates have risen, letting the investor redeem early and reinvest at the new, higher rates"]'::jsonb, 2, 'When rates rise, the bond''s fixed coupon becomes unattractive relative to new issues and its market price falls, so the investor''s put option lets them force redemption at the predetermined price and redeploy the proceeds into higher-yielding bonds. If rates have fallen or credit has improved, the bond''s market price rises above the put price, so an investor would simply sell at the market price rather than exercise a put at a lower fixed price.'),
  ('FI04', 'Fixed Income', 'Bond Features', 'Convertible bond tradeoff', 'A convertible bond allows the holder to exchange the bond for a fixed number of common shares of the issuer. In exchange for this conversion option, investors typically accept:', '["A coupon rate higher than that of an otherwise identical non-convertible bond","A coupon rate lower than that of an otherwise identical non-convertible bond","A shorter maturity than that of an otherwise identical non-convertible bond"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The conversion option gives the holder potential upside if the issuer''s share price rises, and investors pay for that upside by accepting a lower coupon than a plain bond would carry, since the option itself has value. A higher coupon would mean investors are paid extra for a feature that already benefits them, the opposite of how options are priced, and the give-up shows up in the coupon rate, not in a shortened maturity.'),
  ('FI05', 'Fixed Income', 'Bond Features', 'Coupon payment calculation', 'A corporate bond has a face value of $5,000 and an annual coupon rate of 6%, paid semiannually. Each semiannual coupon payment is:', '["$150","$300","$600"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The annual coupon is 5,000 x 6% = $300, and paying semiannually splits that into two equal payments of $150 each. $300 is the full annual coupon before it is split into two payments, and $600 doubles the annual coupon instead of halving it.'),
  ('FI06', 'Fixed Income', 'Bond Features', 'Perpetual bond pricing', 'A perpetual bond has a face value of $1,000 and pays an annual coupon of $70 forever. If investors require a yield of 8% on bonds of this type, the bond''s price is closest to:', '["$875","$470","$1,000"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A perpetuity''s price is simply its level payment divided by the required yield, with no maturity date to discount back to: $70 / 0.08 = $875. The $1,000 figure assumes the bond prices at par regardless of the coupon-versus-yield relationship, an assumption that only holds when the coupon rate equals the required yield (7% does not equal 8% here), and $470 mistakenly treats the bond as a 10-year annuity, forgetting that a perpetual bond has no final maturity date at all.'),
  ('FI07', 'Fixed Income', 'Bond Features', 'Floating-rate coupon reset', 'A floating-rate note pays a coupon equal to a reference rate plus a fixed quoted margin of 120 basis points. At the most recent reset date, the reference rate was 4.30%. The note''s coupon rate for the upcoming period is:', '["4.30%","5.50%","3.10%"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The coupon resets to the reference rate plus the quoted margin: 4.30% + 1.20% = 5.50%. The 4.30% figure just restates the reference rate alone with the margin dropped entirely, and 3.10% subtracts the margin instead of adding it.'),
  ('FI08', 'Fixed Income', 'Yield Measures', 'Current yield calculation', 'A bond has a face value of $1,000, an annual coupon rate of 8%, and is currently trading at a price of $800. The bond''s current yield is closest to:', '["8.00%","10.00%","8.89%"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Current yield divides the annual coupon by the current market price, not the face value: $80 / $800 = 10.00%. The 8.00% figure is just the coupon rate, which equals current yield only when a bond trades at par, and 8.89% divides the coupon by the average of price and face value ($900), a denominator that belongs to an approximate-yield formula, not to current yield.'),
  ('FI09', 'Fixed Income', 'Yield Measures', 'Current yield calculation', 'A bond has a face value of $1,000, an annual coupon rate of 9%, and is currently trading at a price of $1,125. The bond''s current yield is closest to:', '["8.00%","9.00%","8.47%"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Current yield divides the annual coupon by the current market price: $90 / $1,125 = 8.00%. The 9.00% figure is just the coupon rate, which equals current yield only at par, and 8.47% divides the coupon by the average of price and face value ($1,062.50) instead of by the market price alone.'),
  ('FI10', 'Fixed Income', 'Yield Measures', 'Yield to maturity concept', 'The yield to maturity (YTM) on a bond is best described as:', '["The bond''s annual coupon payment divided by its current market price","The single discount rate that equates the present value of all the bond''s future cash flows to its current market price","The rate at which the bond''s price is guaranteed to grow each year regardless of market conditions"]'::jsonb, 1, 'YTM is the bond''s internal rate of return: the one discount rate that, applied to every remaining coupon and the face value repayment, produces exactly the current market price. The first choice describes current yield, a simpler measure that ignores the time value of money and any capital gain or loss to par, and the guaranteed-growth choice confuses YTM with an assured return, when YTM is only realized if the bond is held to maturity and every coupon is reinvested at that same rate.'),
  ('FI11', 'Fixed Income', 'Yield Measures', 'Yield to maturity calculation', 'A bond has a face value of $1,000, a 3-year maturity, and an annual coupon rate of 6% paid annually. The bond is currently priced at $973.76. Its yield to maturity is closest to:', '["6.16%","6.00%","7.00%"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Discounting the three $60 coupons and the $1,000 face value at 7% reproduces the given price of $973.76 exactly, confirming a 7.00% yield to maturity. The 6.16% figure is the current yield ($60 / $973.76), a different and simpler yield measure, and 6.00% is just the coupon rate, which would be the yield only if the bond priced at par -- but a price below face value means the true yield must exceed the coupon rate, not equal it.'),
  ('FI12', 'Fixed Income', 'Yield Measures', 'Yield to maturity calculation', 'A bond has a face value of $1,000, a 4-year maturity, and an annual coupon rate of 8% paid annually. The bond is currently priced at $1,069.30. Its yield to maturity is closest to:', '["7.48%","8.00%","6.00%"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Discounting the four $80 coupons and the $1,000 face value at 6% reproduces the given price of $1,069.30 exactly, confirming a 6.00% yield to maturity. The 7.48% figure is the current yield ($80 / $1,069.30), a different and simpler yield measure, and 8.00% is just the coupon rate -- but a price above face value means the true yield must fall short of the coupon rate, not equal it.'),
  ('FI13', 'Fixed Income', 'Yield Measures', 'Yield spread calculation', 'A corporate bond has a yield to maturity of 6.35%. A Treasury bond of comparable maturity yields 4.10%. The corporate bond''s yield spread over the Treasury benchmark is closest to:', '["22.5 basis points","225 basis points","635 basis points"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The yield spread is simply the difference between the two yields: 6.35% - 4.10% = 2.25%, or 225 basis points. The 22.5 basis point figure misplaces a decimal, forgetting that 1% equals 100 basis points rather than 10, and 635 basis points just restates the corporate bond''s own yield in basis points without ever subtracting the Treasury benchmark.'),
  ('FI14', 'Fixed Income', 'Yield Measures', 'Yield spread interpretation', 'A corporate bond''s yield spread over a comparable-maturity Treasury bond is best interpreted as compensation to the investor for:', '["The risk-free time value of money, which the Treasury yield already excludes","Risks beyond the Treasury benchmark, such as credit risk and liquidity risk, that the corporate bond carries","The stated coupon rate printed on the corporate bond''s certificate"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The spread is the extra yield a corporate bond offers over a benchmark Treasury of similar maturity, compensating investors for risks the Treasury does not carry, primarily credit risk and liquidity risk. The Treasury yield itself already captures the pure time value of money (the opposite of what the first choice claims), and the spread has no direct link to the bond''s own coupon rate, which is just the contractual cash payment schedule, not a measure of risk compensation.'),
  ('FI15', 'Fixed Income', 'Bond Pricing', 'Price-yield inverse relationship', 'As the required market yield on a fixed-rate bond rises, holding all else constant, the bond''s price will:', '["Rise, because a higher yield increases each future cash flow''s nominal value","Fall, because those same future cash flows are discounted at a higher rate","Stay unchanged, because the coupon rate printed on the bond is fixed"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Bond price is the present value of fixed future cash flows, so discounting those unchanged cash flows at a higher required yield produces a lower present value -- price and yield move in opposite directions. The fixed coupon rate does mean the cash flows themselves never change, but that is exactly why the entire adjustment to a new market yield must show up in the price, not in the cash flows.'),
  ('FI16', 'Fixed Income', 'Bond Pricing', 'Premium bond condition', 'A bond''s coupon rate is 7%, and the market yield required by investors for bonds of similar risk and maturity is 5%. This bond will trade at:', '["A premium, because its coupon rate exceeds the market yield","A discount, because its coupon rate exceeds the market yield","Par, because the coupon rate is fixed regardless of the market yield"]'::jsonb, 0, 'When a bond''s coupon rate exceeds the yield investors currently require, its cash flows are more attractive than a newly issued bond''s would be, so investors bid the price above face value until the bond''s actual yield falls in line with the market''s 5% -- a premium. A discount arises in the opposite case, when the coupon rate falls short of the market yield, and par requires the two rates to be equal, not merely for the coupon to be fixed.'),
  ('FI17', 'Fixed Income', 'Bond Pricing', 'At-issuance discount pricing', 'A newly issued bond has a face value of $1,000, an annual coupon rate of 4% paid annually, and a 5-year maturity. The market yield required on bonds of this risk and maturity is 6%. The bond''s issue price is closest to:', '["$916","$1,000","$931"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Discounting each of the five $40 coupons and the $1,000 face value at 6% gives a price of $915.75, closest to $916 -- a discount, as expected since the 4% coupon is below the 6% market yield. The $1,000 figure comes from mistakenly discounting the bond at its own 4% coupon rate instead of the 6% market yield, which always reproduces par regardless of the true yield, and $931 drops one year of discounting, treating this as a 4-year bond instead of 5.'),
  ('FI18', 'Fixed Income', 'Bond Pricing', 'At-issuance premium pricing', 'A bond has a face value of $1,000, an annual coupon rate of 9% paid annually, and a 6-year maturity. The market yield required on bonds of this risk and maturity is 6%. The bond''s price today is closest to:', '["$1,126","$1,185","$1,148"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Discounting each of the six $90 coupons and the $1,000 face value at 6% gives a price of $1,147.52, closest to $1,148 -- a premium, since the 9% coupon exceeds the 6% market yield. The $1,126 figure treats this as a 5-year bond, discounting the face value one year too early, and $1,185 discounts each cash flow with simple interest (dividing by 1 plus rate times t) instead of compounding, understating how much later cash flows should be discounted.'),
  ('FI19', 'Fixed Income', 'Bond Pricing', 'Mid-life discount pricing', 'A bond was issued with a 10-year maturity, an annual coupon rate of 5% paid annually, and a $1,000 face value. Four years have now passed, leaving 6 years until maturity, and the market yield required on bonds of this remaining maturity and risk is now 6%. The bond''s current price is closest to:', '["$951","$926","$1,000"]'::jsonb, 0, 'With 6 years remaining, discounting six $50 coupons and the $1,000 face value at 6% gives a price of $950.83, closest to $951. The $926 figure mistakenly discounts the cash flows over the original 10-year maturity instead of the 6 years actually remaining, and $1,000 comes from discounting at the bond''s own 5% coupon rate instead of the current 6% market yield, which always returns par regardless of the true remaining term.'),
  ('FI20', 'Fixed Income', 'Bond Pricing', 'Semiannual-pay pricing', 'A bond has a face value of $1,000, an annual coupon rate of 8% paid semiannually, and a 3-year maturity. The market yield required on bonds of this risk and maturity is 6% annually, compounded semiannually. The bond''s price is closest to:', '["$902","$1,054","$1,028"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Semiannual pricing requires a $40 coupon each period, a 3% period rate, and 6 total periods; discounting all six $40 coupons and the $1,000 face value at 3% per period gives $1,054.17, closest to $1,054. The $902 figure keeps six periods and the $40 coupon but forgets to halve the discount rate, discounting at 6% per period instead of 3%, and $1,028 correctly uses the 3% period rate and $40 coupon but forgets to double the number of periods, discounting only 3 periods instead of 6.'),
  ('FI21', 'Fixed Income', 'Bond Pricing', 'Par bond condition', 'A bond''s coupon rate exactly equals the market yield required by investors for bonds of similar risk and maturity. This bond will price at:', '["A discount, since the coupon payments alone are insufficient compensation","Par, because the fixed coupon exactly matches what the market currently requires","A premium, since a coupon-paying bond is always worth more than its face value"]'::jsonb, 1, 'When the coupon rate equals the required market yield, discounting the coupons and face value at that same rate always returns exactly the face value, so the bond trades at par. Neither a discount nor a premium can arise here: a discount requires the coupon rate to fall short of the market yield, and a premium requires it to exceed the market yield, and a bond is only worth more than face value once yields fall below the coupon, not as some general rule.'),
  ('FI22', 'Fixed Income', 'Bond Pricing', 'Zero-coupon pricing', 'A zero-coupon bond has a face value of $1,000 and an 8-year maturity. The market yield required on bonds of this risk and maturity is 5%. The bond''s price today is closest to:', '["$714","$645","$677"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A zero-coupon bond''s price is simply the face value discounted back for the full term with no coupons to add: 1,000 / 1.05^8 = $676.84, closest to $677. The $714 figure discounts using simple interest, dividing by (1 + 0.05 x 8) instead of compounding 1.05 to the 8th power, and $645 discounts one year too many, treating this as a 9-year bond instead of 8.'),
  ('FI23', 'Fixed Income', 'Bond Pricing', 'Mid-life premium pricing', 'A bond was issued with an 8-year maturity, an annual coupon rate of 7% paid annually, and a $1,000 face value. Three years have now passed, leaving 5 years until maturity, and the market yield required on bonds of this remaining maturity and risk is now 5%. The bond''s current price is closest to:', '["$1,129","$1,087","$1,071"]'::jsonb, 1, 'With 5 years remaining, discounting five $70 coupons and the $1,000 face value at 5% gives a price of $1,086.59, closest to $1,087. The $1,129 figure mistakenly discounts over the original 8-year maturity instead of the 5 years actually remaining, and $1,071 discounts over only 4 remaining years, one year short of the correct 5.'),
  ('FI24', 'Fixed Income', 'Term Structure and Yield Curve', 'Normal yield curve shape', 'A yield curve in which long-term interest rates are higher than short-term interest rates is described as:', '["Normal (upward-sloping), typically associated with expectations of stable or growing economic activity","Inverted (downward-sloping), typically associated with expectations of an economic slowdown","Flat, indicating that investors expect no change in future short-term rates"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A normal, upward-sloping curve is the most commonly observed shape and is generally associated with investors demanding greater compensation for lending over longer horizons, consistent with expectations of continued or growing economic activity. An inverted curve describes the opposite slope entirely, long-term rates below short-term rates, and a flat curve describes little difference across maturities, neither of which matches a curve where long-term rates sit above short-term rates.'),
  ('FI25', 'Fixed Income', 'Term Structure and Yield Curve', 'Inverted yield curve signal', 'An inverted yield curve, in which short-term interest rates exceed long-term interest rates, is most commonly interpreted by market participants as a signal that:', '["Investors expect an economic slowdown and anticipate future short-term rates to fall","Investors expect robust economic growth and anticipate future short-term rates to rise","The central bank has permanently fixed short-term rates above long-term rates"]'::jsonb, 0, 'An inverted curve is widely watched as a recession signal: it suggests investors expect the central bank to cut short-term rates in the future in response to a slowing economy, pulling average future short-term rates, and therefore long-term yields, below today''s short-term rate. Expecting robust growth is normally associated with a normal, upward-sloping curve instead, and central banks set policy rates but do not fix the entire yield curve''s shape by decree.'),
  ('FI26', 'Fixed Income', 'Term Structure and Yield Curve', 'Flat yield curve signal', 'A flat yield curve, in which short-term and long-term interest rates are approximately equal, most directly suggests that market participants expect:', '["Future short-term interest rates to remain roughly at their current level","A sharp and sustained decline in long-term interest rates only","Corporate bonds to default at the same rate regardless of maturity"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Because longer-term yields largely reflect the market''s expectation of the path of future short-term rates, a flat curve indicates investors see little reason to expect short-term rates to rise or fall meaningfully from current levels. A curve shape reflects a view about interest rates generally, not a prediction confined only to long-term rates, and it says nothing about corporate default rates, which relate to credit risk rather than to the shape of a benchmark yield curve.'),
  ('FI27', 'Fixed Income', 'Term Structure and Yield Curve', 'Forward rate calculation', 'The 1-year spot rate is 4% and the 2-year spot rate is 5%. Using these two spot rates, the implied 1-year forward rate beginning one year from today is closest to:', '["5.00%","0.96%","6.01%"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The forward rate satisfies (1 + S2)^2 = (1 + S1) x (1 + f), so 1 + f = 1.05^2 / 1.04 = 1.060096, giving a forward rate of 6.01%. The 5.00% figure simply restates the 2-year spot rate unchanged, skipping the forward-rate calculation entirely, and 0.96% comes from forgetting to square (1 + S2) before dividing out (1 + S1), understating the compounding the longer spot rate actually represents.'),
  ('FI28', 'Fixed Income', 'Term Structure and Yield Curve', 'Spot rate discounting', 'The 5-year spot rate is 6%. Using this rate, the present value of a single $1,000 cash flow to be received in exactly 5 years is closest to:', '["$747","$769","$792"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A spot rate discounts a single cash flow with ordinary compound discounting: 1,000 / 1.06^5 = $747.26, closest to $747. The $769 figure uses simple interest, dividing by (1 + 0.06 x 5) instead of compounding 1.06 to the 5th power, and $792 discounts for only 4 years instead of the full 5, understating how long the cash flow actually waits to be paid.'),
  ('FI29', 'Fixed Income', 'Term Structure and Yield Curve', 'Spot rate bond pricing', 'A 2-year bond has a face value of $1,000 and an annual coupon rate of 6% paid annually. The 1-year spot rate is 4% and the 2-year spot rate is 5%. Using these spot rates to discount each cash flow individually, the bond''s price is closest to:', '["$961","$1,019","$1,038"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Each cash flow must be discounted at the spot rate matching its own maturity: 60 / 1.04 + 1,060 / 1.05^2 = 57.69 + 961.45 = $1,019.14, closest to $1,019. The $1,038 figure discounts both the year-1 and year-2 cash flows at the 1-year spot rate, ignoring that the year-2 cash flow should use the longer 2-year spot rate instead, and $961 drops the year-1 coupon entirely, discounting only the year-2 cash flow of $1,060.'),
  ('FI30', 'Fixed Income', 'Duration and Convexity', 'Macaulay duration concept', 'Macaulay duration is best described as:', '["The bond''s time to maturity, expressed in years","The percentage change in a bond''s price for a 1% change in yield","The present-value-weighted average time until the bond''s cash flows are received"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Macaulay duration weights the time to each remaining cash flow by that cash flow''s share of the bond''s total present value, producing a single weighted-average time figure, typically shorter than the bond''s stated maturity whenever coupons are paid before the final period. Time to maturity is just one input into that calculation, not duration itself, and the percentage-price-change-per-unit-yield description belongs to modified duration, a related but distinct measure derived from Macaulay duration.'),
  ('FI31', 'Fixed Income', 'Duration and Convexity', 'Macaulay duration calculation', 'A newly issued bond has a face value of $1,000, a 3-year maturity, and an annual coupon rate of 5% paid annually, priced today at exactly $1,000 because its 5% coupon equals the 5% market yield. The bond''s Macaulay duration is closest to:', '["2.86 years","2.00 years","3.00 years"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Macaulay duration is the present-value-weighted average time to each cash flow: (1 x 47.62 + 2 x 45.35 + 3 x 907.03) / 1,000 = 2,859.41 / 1,000 = 2.86 years. The 2.00 figure is a naive, unweighted average of the three payment dates that ignores how much larger the final cash flow''s present value is, and 3.00 years mistakes duration for the bond''s maturity, a relationship that holds only for a zero-coupon bond, which has just a single cash flow to weight.'),
  ('FI32', 'Fixed Income', 'Duration and Convexity', 'Modified duration conversion', 'A bond has a Macaulay duration of 4.5 years and a yield to maturity of 8%, compounded annually. Its modified duration is closest to:', '["4.17 years","4.50 years","2.50 years"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Modified duration equals Macaulay duration divided by (1 + yield / periods per year): 4.5 / 1.08 = 4.17 years. The 4.50 figure just reports the Macaulay duration unchanged, skipping the conversion entirely, and 2.50 comes from misplacing a decimal in the yield, dividing by 1.8 (as if the yield were 80%) instead of 1.08.'),
  ('FI33', 'Fixed Income', 'Duration and Convexity', 'Modified duration conversion', 'A bond has a Macaulay duration of 6.2 years and a yield to maturity of 7%, compounded semiannually. Its modified duration is closest to:', '["5.79 years","6.20 years","5.99 years"]'::jsonb, 2, 'With semiannual compounding, modified duration divides Macaulay duration by (1 + yield / 2), not by (1 + yield): 6.2 / 1.035 = 5.99 years. The 5.79 figure mistakenly divides by (1 + yield) using the annual convention, ignoring that this bond compounds semiannually, and 6.20 simply reports the Macaulay duration unchanged, skipping the conversion to modified duration entirely.'),
  ('FI34', 'Fixed Income', 'Duration and Convexity', 'Duration-based price change', 'A bond has a modified duration of 6.5. If the market yield on this bond rises by 50 basis points, the estimated percentage change in the bond''s price is closest to:', '["+3.25%","-3.25%","-32.50%"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The duration-based price change estimate is the negative of modified duration times the yield change: -6.5 x 0.50% = -3.25%, a price decrease, since yield and price move in opposite directions. The +3.25% figure gets the correct magnitude but the wrong sign, forgetting that a yield increase must produce a price decrease, and -32.50% misreads 50 basis points as 5% instead of 0.50%, overstating the yield change by a factor of ten.'),
  ('FI35', 'Fixed Income', 'Duration and Convexity', 'Duration-based price change', 'A bond has a Macaulay duration of 8.0 years and a yield to maturity of 6%, compounded semiannually. If the market yield falls by 75 basis points, the estimated percentage change in the bond''s price is closest to:', '["-5.83%","+6.00%","+5.83%"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Modified duration is first found as 8.0 / (1 + 0.06/2) = 8.0 / 1.03 = 7.77, and the price change estimate is the negative of modified duration times the yield change: -7.77 x (-0.75%) = +5.83%, a price increase, since a yield decrease raises price. The -5.83% figure gets the magnitude right but flips the sign, forgetting that a yield decrease must raise price, not lower it, and +6.00% skips the Macaulay-to-modified conversion entirely, multiplying the yield change by the unconverted 8.0 Macaulay figure instead of the correct 7.77 modified duration.'),
  ('FI36', 'Fixed Income', 'Duration and Convexity', 'Duration and coupon rate', 'Holding maturity and yield constant, a bond with a higher coupon rate will have, relative to an otherwise identical lower-coupon bond:', '["A lower Macaulay duration, because higher coupons return more cash flow earlier, before maturity","A higher Macaulay duration, because higher coupons increase the bond''s total cash flow","The same Macaulay duration, since duration depends only on maturity, not on the coupon rate"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A higher coupon shifts relatively more of the bond''s total present value into the earlier coupon payments and relatively less into the final face-value repayment, pulling the present-value-weighted average time to cash flow earlier and lower. The higher-total-cash-flow reasoning confuses the sheer dollar amount of cash returned with the timing-weighted average that duration actually measures, and duration is clearly sensitive to the coupon rate, not just to maturity -- a zero-coupon bond, the lowest possible coupon, has the highest duration of all for a given maturity, equal to that maturity itself.'),
  ('FI37', 'Fixed Income', 'Duration and Convexity', 'Convexity-adjusted price change', 'A bond has a modified duration of 7.0 and a convexity of 85. If the bond''s yield rises by 200 basis points, the convexity-adjusted estimate of the percentage change in the bond''s price is closest to:', '["-14.00%","-12.30%","-15.70%"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The convexity-adjusted estimate adds a correction term to the linear duration estimate: -7.0 x 2.00% + 0.5 x 85 x (2.00%)^2 = -14.00% + 1.70% = -12.30%. The -14.00% figure is the duration-only estimate with the convexity correction left out entirely, overstating how much the price actually falls, and -15.70% subtracts the convexity term instead of adding it, compounding the duration estimate''s overstatement rather than correcting it.'),
  ('FI38', 'Fixed Income', 'Duration and Convexity', 'Convexity effect on estimates', 'A modified-duration-only estimate of a bond''s percentage price change for a given yield change tends to:', '["Underestimate the bond''s actual price increase for a yield decrease, and overestimate its actual price decline for a yield increase","Overestimate both the price increase for a yield decrease and the price decline for a yield increase","Provide an exact price change for any size of yield movement, since bond pricing is linear in yield"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Because the true price-yield relationship is convex rather than linear, the straight-line duration estimate understates the actual price gain when yields fall and overstates the actual price loss when yields rise; adding the convexity term corrects the estimate in both directions. The bond price-yield relationship is curved, not linear, so a duration-only estimate is never exact except for infinitesimally small yield changes, and it does not systematically overestimate in both directions -- it errs in opposite ways depending on which direction yields move.'),
  ('FI39', 'Fixed Income', 'Credit Risk and Ratings', 'Seniority spread comparison', 'A single issuer''s senior secured bonds trade at a spread of 150 basis points over the benchmark, while its subordinated unsecured bonds of similar maturity trade at a spread of 310 basis points. The incremental spread investors demand specifically for holding the subordinated bonds instead of the senior bonds is closest to:', '["310 basis points","460 basis points","160 basis points"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The incremental compensation for subordination is the difference between the two spreads: 310 - 150 = 160 basis points. The 460 basis point figure adds the two spreads together instead of subtracting them, and 310 basis points reports the subordinated bond''s entire spread as if none of it reflected the same baseline, issuer-specific risk that the senior bond''s own 150 basis point spread already captures.'),
  ('FI40', 'Fixed Income', 'Credit Risk and Ratings', 'Downgrade effect on spread', 'A corporate bond is downgraded by a major rating agency, with no change in the benchmark risk-free rate. All else equal, this downgrade should cause the bond''s:', '["Yield spread to narrow and its price to rise","Yield spread to stay unchanged, since ratings do not affect trading prices","Yield spread to widen and its price to fall"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A downgrade signals higher perceived credit risk, so investors demand a wider spread over the risk-free benchmark to keep holding the bond, and since price and yield move in opposite directions, that wider required yield pushes the bond''s price down. A narrowing spread and rising price would describe an upgrade, the opposite event, and ratings changes routinely move market prices in practice, which is exactly why they are watched so closely.'),
  ('FI41', 'Fixed Income', 'Credit Risk and Ratings', 'Spread widening plus rate move', 'A bond has a modified duration of 4.0. Its benchmark rate rises by 30 basis points at the same time its credit spread widens by 50 basis points following a rating downgrade. The estimated total percentage change in the bond''s price is closest to:', '["-1.20%","-2.00%","-3.20%"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Both yield-change sources add together before applying duration: a 30 basis point benchmark increase plus a 50 basis point spread widening is an 80 basis point total yield increase, so the price change estimate is -4.0 x 0.80% = -3.20%. The -2.00% figure uses only the spread widening and ignores the benchmark move entirely, and -1.20% uses only the benchmark move and ignores the spread widening, each capturing just one of the two yield-change sources instead of their combined effect.'),
  ('FI42', 'Fixed Income', 'Credit Risk and Ratings', 'Spread widening and price impact', 'A bond has a modified duration of 5.0. Following a rating downgrade, its credit spread widens by 80 basis points while the benchmark rate stays unchanged. The estimated percentage change in the bond''s price is closest to:', '["-4.00%","+4.00%","-0.40%"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A spread widening is a yield increase from this bond''s perspective, so the duration-based estimate applies directly: -5.0 x 0.80% = -4.00%. The +4.00% figure gets the magnitude right but the wrong sign, forgetting that a wider spread raises the bond''s required yield and therefore lowers its price, and -0.40% misreads 80 basis points as 8 basis points, dropping a zero and understating the yield change tenfold.'),
  ('FI43', 'Fixed Income', 'Credit Risk and Ratings', 'Investment-grade vs high-yield', 'Compared to an investment-grade bond, a high-yield (non-investment-grade) bond of similar maturity typically exhibits:', '["A lower yield spread over the benchmark, reflecting its lower credit risk","A higher yield spread over the benchmark, reflecting its greater risk of default","No difference in yield spread, since credit ratings do not affect market pricing"]'::jsonb, 1, 'High-yield bonds carry materially greater default risk than investment-grade bonds, and the market prices that risk into a wider yield spread over the benchmark to compensate investors for bearing it. A lower spread would describe safer, not riskier, bonds, and credit ratings are directly reflected in market pricing -- a rating change routinely moves a bond''s spread and price, the opposite of having no effect.'),
  ('FI44', 'Fixed Income', 'Credit Risk and Ratings', 'Investment-grade threshold', 'A bond rated BBB- by Standard and Poor''s (or Baa3 by Moody''s) sits at the boundary between:', '["High-yield status above this rating and investment-grade status below it","Two equivalent investment-grade sub-categories, with no meaningful distinction between them","Investment-grade status at or above this rating and high-yield (speculative-grade) status below it"]'::jsonb, 2, 'BBB-/Baa3 is the lowest rating still classified as investment-grade; any rating below this threshold is classified high-yield or speculative-grade, a distinction that matters enormously for institutional mandates restricted to investment-grade debt. The first choice has the boundary''s direction backwards, since BBB-/Baa3 and above is investment-grade, not high-yield, and the two categories are far from equivalent -- crossing from investment-grade into high-yield typically triggers forced selling by investment-grade-only mandates and a material repricing.'),
  ('FI45', 'Fixed Income', 'Securitization and ABS', 'Pass-through mechanics', 'A mortgage pass-through security works by:', '["Guaranteeing a fixed coupon and fixed maturity date regardless of homeowner behavior","Collecting principal and interest from a pool of mortgages and passing those cash flows through to investors, minus servicing fees","Paying investors only the interest portion of mortgage payments, with principal retained by the issuer"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A pass-through security pools many individual mortgages and forwards the combined principal and interest collected from homeowners to investors each period, less a servicing fee, so investor cash flow directly mirrors what the underlying borrowers actually pay. Because homeowners can prepay their mortgages early, neither the effective yield nor the effective maturity is truly fixed the way the first choice claims, and investors receive both principal and interest, not interest alone.'),
  ('FI46', 'Fixed Income', 'Securitization and ABS', 'Prepayment risk direction', 'Holders of mortgage pass-through securities face prepayment risk primarily because, when interest rates fall:', '["Homeowners are contractually forced to prepay their mortgages at the issuer''s discretion","Homeowners refinance more often, returning investor principal early to be reinvested at the new, lower rates","Scheduled monthly payments stop entirely until rates rise again"]'::jsonb, 1, 'When rates fall, refinancing into a cheaper mortgage becomes attractive to homeowners, so prepayments accelerate and investors receive principal back earlier than expected, forcing them to reinvest that principal at the new, lower prevailing rates -- the core of prepayment risk. Homeowners choose to prepay based on their own economic incentives, not at the issuer''s discretion, and scheduled payments do not simply stop; if anything, prepayment activity increases as rates fall, not decreases.'),
  ('FI47', 'Fixed Income', 'Securitization and ABS', 'Extension risk', 'When interest rates rise, holders of mortgage pass-through securities are most likely to experience:', '["Faster-than-expected prepayments, shortening the security''s effective life","No change in prepayment behavior, since prepayment speed depends only on the mortgage rate at origination","Slower-than-expected prepayments, extending the security''s effective life beyond what was anticipated"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Rising rates make refinancing unattractive, so homeowners prepay less than expected and the pass-through''s average life extends further into the future than investors originally anticipated -- extension risk, the mirror image of the prepayment risk that appears when rates fall. Faster prepayment is exactly what happens when rates fall, not rise, and prepayment speed responds to the gap between the mortgage''s rate and current market refinancing rates, not just to the rate fixed at origination.'),
  ('FI48', 'Fixed Income', 'Securitization and ABS', 'Tranche loss allocation', 'A securitization has a $100 million senior tranche and a $20 million subordinate tranche. If credit losses on the underlying collateral pool total $26 million, the loss absorbed by the senior tranche is closest to:', '["$26 million","$0","$6 million"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The subordinate tranche absorbs losses first, up to its full $20 million size, so the senior tranche only absorbs the excess: $26 million - $20 million = $6 million. The $26 million figure ignores the subordinate tranche''s protection entirely, exposing the senior tranche to the full loss, and $0 assumes the subordinate tranche can absorb any loss no matter how large, forgetting that its protection is capped at its own $20 million size.'),
  ('FI49', 'Fixed Income', 'Securitization and ABS', 'Weighted average coupon', 'A mortgage pool consists of $60 million in loans with a 6.0% coupon and $40 million in loans with a 7.5% coupon. The pool''s weighted average coupon is closest to:', '["6.60%","6.75%","7.50%"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The weighted average coupon weights each loan group by its share of the pool: (60/100 x 6.0%) + (40/100 x 7.5%) = 3.60% + 3.00% = 6.60%. The 6.75% figure is the simple, unweighted average of the two coupon rates, ignoring that the 6.0% group makes up a larger 60% share of the pool, and 7.50% just reports the higher of the two coupons alone, as if the entire pool were made up of that loan group.'),
  ('FI50', 'Fixed Income', 'Securitization and ABS', 'Pass-through cash flow calculation', 'A mortgage pass-through pool has an outstanding balance of $50 million and a pass-through rate of 6% paid to investors annually but distributed monthly. The interest portion of the monthly cash flow to investors is closest to:', '["$3,000,000","$250,000","$300,000"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Monthly interest passed through to investors is the annual rate applied to the outstanding balance, divided by 12: $50,000,000 x 6% / 12 = $250,000. The $3,000,000 figure is the full annual interest amount with the monthly conversion left out entirely, and $300,000 applies a monthly conversion but divides by 10 instead of 12.');
